{{$NEXT}}
  - Fix RBAC-aware tool descriptions being served only by run_stdio.
    _update_tool_descriptions was called from that one place, so every
    other way of serving the same object — the Net::Async::MCP path
    documented in README, an embedded to_stdio, and the to_action HTTP
    transport inherited from MCP::Server — handed the LLM the static
    descriptions with no word about which resources exist per namespace.
    Permission checks were never affected, only what the model was told.
    _tools is now overridden, which is the single funnel every transport
    passes on tools/list and tools/call, and it delegates to SUPER so the
    list copy and the 'tools' event cannot drift. Construction stays
    lazy: nothing touches the API until a request arrives.
    Note: on a server whose api cannot be built at all, tools/list now
    answers with a JSON-RPC internal error where it previously returned
    the static list. tools/call already failed that way.
  - Fix k8s_get output => 'yaml' emitting invalid YAML. It dumped
    TO_JSON through YAML::XS, which does not know JSON::PP::Boolean and
    wrote "hostNetwork: !!perl/scalar:JSON::PP::Boolean 1" instead of
    "hostNetwork: true" — not a manifest kubectl would accept, for every
    boolean field of every resource. It now calls IO::K8s's own
    to_yaml(), which serialises booleans correctly. The optional
    YAML::XS dependency is gone with it, and so is its silent fallback
    to JSON: an object that cannot render as YAML now says so instead of
    quietly answering in the wrong format.
  - k8s_patch takes an optional subresource parameter. With
    subresource => 'status' the call goes to Kubernetes::REST's
    patch_status() and the patch type defaults to merge instead of
    strategic, because custom resources answer a strategic merge patch
    with 415; an explicit patch_type still wins. Without it, a status
    write was silently lost: the API server strips status from any write
    to the main endpoint of a resource that has a status subresource and
    answers 2xx anyway, so the tool reported success and stored nothing.
    RBAC is checked on <plural>/status, not <plural> — a patch grant on
    the main endpoint does not hand out status writes. Tool count is
    unchanged at 10.
  - Fix RBAC discovery dropping subresources. _discover_namespace kept
    only pods/log and threw away every other subresource before it
    reached the rules map, so a correctly narrow Role granting e.g.
    patch on deployments/status was denied — a permission denial that
    was not one. Subresources are now stored as the API server reports
    them; they cannot collide with a base plural, which never contains
    a '/'. allowed_resources still filters them, so tool descriptions
    are byte-identical.
  - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE from the subresource fix above: a namespace whose
    only grants are subresources now appears in allowed_namespaces,
    because discover() stores a namespace only if it has any rules at
    all and such a namespace used to end up empty. If that makes more
    than one namespace reachable, _resolve_namespace stops auto-filling
    and tools ask for an explicit namespace instead of guessing. Safe
    direction, but visible on an existing setup.
  - k8s_logs reads the pod's log subresource through Kubernetes::REST's
    public log() instead of hand-building the URL and calling the
    private _request(). One consequence: an HTTP error is now raised by
    the client, so a 404 arrives as "Failed to get logs for pod/<name>:
    ... 404 ..." rather than "Error getting logs: 404 ...". Status code
    and API reason are still in the message.
  - Plural lookup tier 2 no longer triggers a full /openapi/v2 download.
    expand_class() resolves through Kubernetes::REST's lazy resource_map,
    which defaults to loading from the cluster, so a single lookup pulled
    the whole OpenAPI spec — and then returned undef anyway, because no
    class shipped with IO::K8s 1.107 defines resource_plural(). The tier
    is skipped when the client would have to fetch, and still runs for a
    CRD provider class registered in a local resource map. Same plurals,
    fewer requests.
  - Move k8s_apply's create-conflict detection into _is_conflict_error
    and document why it stays a regex: Kubernetes::REST 1.107 croaks a
    plain string and carries typed errors only in its deprecated v0
    modules, so there is nothing structured to test. Narrowing the match
    to the current croak format would turn any upstream rewording into a
    false negative. Added regression tests, including one that a 403 on
    create is not mistaken for a conflict.
  - Fix Moo predicate traps in _build_api: env-only MCP_K8S_TOKEN,
    MCP_K8S_SERVER and MCP_K8S_CONTEXT are now honoured at all three
    auth tiers. The has_token / has_server_endpoint / has_context_name
    predicates stay false on a lazy default-only attribute until the
    reader fires, so the previous gate silently dropped env-only
    settings (closes #1).
  - Add t/09-auth-regression.t with 19 subtests covering the predicate
    traps across all three auth tiers, including env-only, empty-string,
    constructor-arg and predicate-state-after-build variants.
  - Bump dependency minimums: MCP 0.06 -> 0.15, Kubernetes::REST and
    IO::K8s 1.002 -> 1.107.
  - The Net::Async::MCP example in README and in the Langertha demo
    script needs Net::Async::MCP 0.004 or newer as a consequence of the
    MCP 0.15 bump. MCP 0.15 implements the stateless protocol revision
    2026-07-28, where every request carries its protocol version in
    params._meta; 0.003 still sends the older handshake and fails with
    "-32602 Missing protocol version". Not a dependency of this
    distribution — the stdio server and mcp-k8s are unaffected.
  - Add Claude Code project tooling (CLAUDE.md, .claude/agents,
    .claude/rules, .claude/skills, .gitignore patterns).

0.002     2026-03-05 21:29:05Z

  - MCP::K8s now inherits from MCP::Server instead of composing it
  - Backward compatible: ->server returns $self
  - Lazy permission discovery: RBAC discovery no longer runs at
    construction time, only when actually needed (first tool call
    or run_stdio). Allows creating MCP::K8s instances without a
    cluster connection.
  - Tool descriptions are updated dynamically after discovery
    instead of eagerly during BUILD

0.001     2026-02-24 02:12:29Z

  - Initial release
  - RBAC-aware MCP server for Kubernetes
    - Dynamic tool discovery via SelfSubjectRulesReview
    - Dynamic tool descriptions reflecting actual permissions
    - Wildcard handling for verbs and resources
    - Permission check before every tool call
  - 10 MCP tools:
    - k8s_permissions — show RBAC permissions
    - k8s_list — list resources with label/field selectors
    - k8s_get — get resource in summary, JSON, or YAML format
    - k8s_create — create a resource from manifest
    - k8s_patch — strategic merge, JSON merge, or JSON patch
    - k8s_delete — delete a resource
    - k8s_logs — pod container logs via raw API endpoint
    - k8s_events — dedicated event listing with object/field filters
    - k8s_rollout_restart — rolling restart for Deployments,
      StatefulSets, DaemonSets (same as kubectl rollout restart)
    - k8s_apply — create-or-update semantics (like kubectl apply),
      tries create first, falls back to strategic merge patch on 409
  - Three authentication methods (tried in order):
    - Direct token via MCP_K8S_TOKEN + MCP_K8S_SERVER
    - In-cluster service account auto-detection
    - Kubeconfig fallback with MCP_K8S_CONTEXT support
  - Dynamic resource plural discovery via API server endpoints
    - 4-tier lookup: static map, IO::K8s class, API discovery, heuristic
    - Supports CRDs (e.g. Cilium) without configuration
  - Auto-namespace detection (single namespace auto-fill,
    in-cluster namespace from mounted service account)
  - Example RBAC manifests in examples/:
    - readonly-serviceaccount.yaml
    - deployer-serviceaccount.yaml
    - full-ops-serviceaccount.yaml
  - Live demo script examples/raider-configmap-demo.pl
    (Langertha::Raider + MCP::K8s)
  - MCP::Kubernetes alias module for CPAN discoverability
